{"summary": "GAAIA discussion-draft comment package (work order ACIA-POL-2026-117): formal comment letter from ACIA to Reps. Obernolte and Trahan (1,685 words, signed by President & CEO Grant Holloway), E&C one-pager (406 words, 5 asks), section-by-section asks table (21 rows), 5 launch-day social posts (all under 280 characters), 151-word member newsletter blurb, and staff Q&A answering the four anticipated Hill questions (all answers under 120 words).

Positions follow the board-approved messaging guide v4 in full: support Sec. 121 preemption and make it permanent (strike the three-year sunset); strike the Sec. 111(f)-(g) critical-safety-incident reporting mandate and the Sec. 101 "catastrophic risk"/"critical safety incident" definitions as unfounded, speculative doomsday-scenario mandates that nationalize the state burdens preemption lifts; strike Sec. 102 CAISI codification, the $100 million annual authorization and fee authority, and ask appropriators to defund CAISI and return NIST to core measurement science; keep frameworks and IVO audits voluntary; support the bill's fraud, impersonation, testbed, NAIRR, CISA and open-source security provisions. The letter leads with the August 2026 member compliance-cost survey (aggregate only, no member companies named) and uses approved language: "unfounded," "speculative doomsday scenarios," "regulatory capture," "one national standard," "patchwork."", "deliverables": ["/workspace/out/comment_letter_GAAIA.md", "/workspace/out/hill_one_pager.md", "/workspace/out/asks_by_section.md", "/workspace/out/social_posts.md", "/workspace/out/newsletter_blurb.md", "/workspace/out/staff_qa.md"], "notes_for_reviewers": "Fact-checking: all section numbers and provision details verified against the sponsors' section-by-section summary (Secs. 101, 102, 111(a)-(g), 112, 113, 121, 122, 123, 131, 132, 141, 301, 311, 321, 401, Title IV incl. NAIRR), the June 4, 2026 press release, Roll Call, ArentFox Schiff, FPF and Instawhat analyses of the draft text. Background facts verified: SB 53 (15-day Cal OES reporting; effective Jan. 1, 2026, via California leginfo), amended NY RAISE Act (72-hour reporting; compliance from January 2027, via Hochul's office), White House National Policy Framework for AI of March 20, 2026 (preemption of "undue burdens," via whitehouse.gov), CAISI's 2025 origin as a rebrand of the U.S. AI Safety Institute (Commerce), $100M/yr FY2027-29 authorization, IVO licensing, 24-hour law-enforcement clock, and $1 million-per-day penalties.

Judgment calls to flag: (1) The messaging guide takes explicit positions on preemption, incident reporting and CAISI; positions on Sec. 111(a)-(e) (make voluntary), Sec. 112 (strike or make voluntary), Sec. 113 (support) and the innovation provisions (support) are derived from the guide's core frame plus our March 2025 OSTP comment ("keep standards voluntary and industry-led") and are marked as such in asks_by_section.md. (2) The letter is dated Oct. 30, 2026 for transmission the week after CEO sign-off; each file carries a draft header noting it must not be sent from this workspace. (3) Survey figures are cited in aggregate only, per policy; no member company is named anywhere in the package."}
